export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"In the series AABABCABCDABCDE..., which letter occupies the 100th position?","A":"H","B":"I","C":"J","D":"K","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["Series","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The series is formed by adding one more letter in each successive block:\nA\nAB\nABC\nABCD\nABCDE ...\nThe length of the blocks is therefore 1, 2, 3, 4, 5...\nThe cumulative number of letters after 13 blocks is:\n1 + 2 + ... + 13 = 91\nThe 100th position is therefore in the 14th block.\nPosition within the 14th block = 100 − 91 = 9, giving I.","revisionPoints":"Recognize triangular-number patterns in series questions.\nSum of first n natural numbers = n(n+1)/2.\nLocate the block containing the required position before identifying the letter.\nSequence questions often require identifying the underlying construction rather than directly counting."},
{"no":2,"text":"What is the number of terms in the series 117, 120, 123, 126, ..., 333?","A":"72","B":"73","C":"76","D":"79","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Arithmetic Progression","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"This is an arithmetic progression.\nFirst term = 117\nCommon difference = 3\nLast term = 333\nUsing:\n\\(a_n = a + (n-1)d\\)\n\\(333 = 117 + (n-1)3\\)\nTherefore, n = 73.","revisionPoints":"Arithmetic progression → constant common difference.\nNumber of terms:\nn = [(last − first) / common difference] + 1\nAlways include both the first and last terms in counting.\nHere the common difference is 3."},
{"no":3,"text":"In how many different ways can four books A, B, C and D be arranged one above another such that the books A and B are never in continuous position?","A":"9","B":"12","C":"14","D":"18","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Permutations","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Total arrangements of four books = 4! = 24.\nConsider A and B as one block when they are together.\nThe block can be arranged as AB or BA, giving 2 possibilities.\nArrangements of the block with C and D = 3! × 2 = 12.\nTherefore, arrangements where A and B are together = 12.\nRequired arrangements = 24 − 12 = 12.","revisionPoints":"Total arrangements of n distinct objects = n!\nFor two objects required to be together:\nTreat them as a single block.\nMultiply by their internal arrangements.\n“Never together” → generally use total − together.\n4! = 24."},
{"no":4,"text":"Carpenter A can make a chair in 6 hours, carpenter B in 7 hours and carpenter C in 8 hours. If each carpenter works for 8 hours per day, how many chairs will be made in 21 days?","A":"61","B":"67","C":"73","D":"79","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Work and Time","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"A's daily production = 8/6 chairs.\nB's daily production = 8/7 chairs.\nC's daily production = 8/8 = 1 chair.\nCombined daily production:\n\\(8/6 + 8/7 + 1\\)\nFor 21 days:\n\\(21 × (8/6 + 8/7 + 1) = 73\\) chairs approximately/exactly as framed by the source.","revisionPoints":"Work-rate = 1 / time required for one unit.\nIf working time is given, output = working time × rate.\nAdd individual rates when workers operate simultaneously.\nKeep units consistent throughout the calculation."},
{"no":5,"text":"A person purchases 100 pens at a discount of 10%. The net amount spent is ₹600. Selling expenses are 15% on the net cost price. What should be the selling price for 100 pens to earn a profit of 25%?","A":"₹802.50","B":"₹811.25","C":"₹862.50","D":"₹875","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Profit and Loss","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Net purchase cost = ₹600.\nSelling expenses = 15% of ₹600:\n₹90\nTotal effective cost = ₹600 + ₹90 = ₹690.\nRequired profit = 25% of ₹690:\n₹172.50\nSelling price = ₹690 + ₹172.50 = ₹862.50.","revisionPoints":"Effective cost includes relevant selling expenses.\nProfit = 25% of total cost, not merely purchase price.\nTotal cost = purchase cost + applicable expenses.\nAlways identify the base on which a percentage is calculated."},
{"no":6,"text":"A schoolteacher has to select the maximum possible number of different groups of 3 students out of a total of 6 students. In how many groups will any particular student be included?","A":"6","B":"8","C":"10","D":"12","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Combinatorics","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Total students = 6.\nFix one particular student.\nTwo more students have to be selected from the remaining 5 students.\nNumber of groups:\n\\(^{5}C_{2} = 5×4/2 = 10\\)\nTherefore, the particular student appears in 10 groups.","revisionPoints":"Combination is used when order does not matter.\nFormula:\n\\(^{n}C_r = n!/[r!(n-r)!]\\)\nIf one member is fixed, select the remaining members from the others.\nHere: 5C2 = 10."},
{"no":7,"text":"In an examination, 70% of the students passed in Paper I and 60% passed in Paper II. 15% failed in both papers while 270 students passed in both papers. What is the total number of students?","A":"600","B":"580","C":"560","D":"540","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Percentages","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Let total students = N.\nFailed in both = 15%, so passed in at least one paper = 85%.\nPassed in Paper I + passed in Paper II − passed in both = passed in at least one.\nTherefore:\n70% + 60% − 85% = 45%\nThus 45% of N = 270.\n\\(N = 270/0.45 = 600\\).","revisionPoints":"Union of two groups:\nA ∪ B = A + B − A ∩ B\n“Failed in both” is the complement of “passed in at least one.”\nPercentage-based set problems can be solved using Venn-diagram logic.\nTotal students = 600."},
{"no":8,"text":"What is Bisphenol A (BPA)?","A":"A medical test for detecting cancer","B":"A test for testing the use of drugs to improve performance by athletes","C":"A chemical used for the development of food-packaging materials","D":"A special type of alloy steel","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Bisphenol A","Chemicals","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Bisphenol A (BPA) is an industrial chemical.\nIt has been used in manufacturing certain plastics and resins.\nIt has historically been associated with some food and beverage packaging materials.\nThe question tests awareness of an environmental/chemical term.","revisionPoints":"BPA → industrial chemical\nAssociated with polycarbonate plastics and epoxy resins.\nFood-contact materials are an important context for BPA discussions.\nChemical exposure and public-health regulation are related policy issues."},
{"no":9,"text":"Which of the following pairs about India's economic indicator and agricultural production are correctly matched?\n1. GDP per capita (current prices): ₹37,000\n2. Rice: 180 million tons\n3. Wheat: 75 million tons\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Economic Indicators","Agricultural Production","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies statements 1 and 3 as correctly matched.\nThe rice figure given in statement 2 is not the correct figure under the source's data.\nThe question is based on rounded economic and agricultural statistics.","revisionPoints":"GDP per capita can be expressed using current prices or other measures.\nAgricultural production figures should always be associated with the relevant year.\nRice and wheat are major food-grain crops in India.\nBe careful with rounded historical data in source-based questions."},
{"no":10,"text":"March 1, 2008 was Saturday. Which day was March 1, 2002?","A":"Thursday","B":"Friday","C":"Saturday","D":"Sunday","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["Calendar","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"From March 1, 2002 to March 1, 2008 there are six years.\nLeap years occurring in the interval contribute an additional day.\nThe total shift in weekdays gives Friday for March 1, 2002 when March 1, 2008 is Saturday.","revisionPoints":"Ordinary year → 365 days = 1 weekday shift.\nLeap year → 366 days = 2 weekday shift.\nFebruary 29 must be considered when calculating date differences.\nCalendar questions require careful counting of leap years."},
{"no":11,"text":"In August 2006, the Government of India notified the Rural Electrification Policy. This policy aimed at providing access to all households by which year?","A":"2008","B":"2009","C":"2010","D":"2012","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Rural Electrification Policy","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Rural Electrification Policy, 2006 set a target for providing electricity access to all households by 2009, according to the source.\nRural electrification was aimed at improving household access and rural development.\nElectricity access is an important component of infrastructure development.","revisionPoints":"Rural Electrification Policy → 2006\nSource target → all households by 2009.\nRural electrification supports education, healthcare, communication and economic activity.\nInfrastructure policies often contain specific target years."},
{"no":12,"text":"As per India's National Population Policy, 2000, by which year was the long-term objective of achieving population stabilization to be achieved?","A":"2025","B":"2035","C":"2045","D":"2055","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Policies","tags":["National Population Policy 2000","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The National Population Policy, 2000 identified population stabilisation as a long-term objective.\nThe target year given in the source is 2045.\nPopulation stabilisation is connected with fertility, mortality, reproductive health and demographic transition.","revisionPoints":"National Population Policy → 2000\nLong-term population stabilisation target → 2045.\nPopulation dynamics depend on fertility, mortality and migration.\nDemographic transition affects population growth over time."},
{"no":13,"text":"Which one of the following Union Ministries is implementing the Biodiesel Mission as the Nodal Ministry?","A":"Ministry of Agriculture","B":"Ministry of Science and Technology","C":"Ministry of New and Renewable Energy","D":"Ministry of Rural Development","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["Biodiesel Mission","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies the Ministry of New and Renewable Energy as the nodal ministry.\nBiodiesel is a biofuel that can be produced from vegetable oils and other biological feedstocks.\nBiofuels are considered an alternative or supplementary energy source.","revisionPoints":"Biodiesel → biofuel\nSource nodal ministry → Ministry of New and Renewable Energy.\nBiofuels can contribute to diversification of energy sources.\nCommon feedstocks include vegetable oils and other biomass-derived materials."},
{"no":14,"text":"Consider the following statements with reference to the Indira Gandhi National Old Age Pension Scheme (IGNOAPS):\n1. All persons of 60 years or above belonging to households below the poverty line in rural areas are eligible.\n2. Central assistance under this scheme is ₹300 per month per beneficiary, and States have been urged to give matching amounts.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Indira Gandhi National Old Age Pension Scheme","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source marks both statements as correct.\nThe scheme provides social-security assistance to eligible elderly persons belonging to economically vulnerable households.\nCentral assistance and State contributions form part of the scheme's financial structure as framed in the question.","revisionPoints":"IGNOAPS → old-age social security\nTarget group → eligible elderly persons from BPL households under the source's framework.\nIt is part of India's social-assistance programmes.\nGovernment welfare schemes often involve Central and State contributions."},
{"no":15,"text":"What is the name of the scheme which provides training and skills to women in traditional and non-traditional trades?","A":"Kishori Shakti Yojana","B":"Rashtriya Mahila Kosh","C":"Swayamsiddha","D":"Swawlamban","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["Swawlamban","Women Empowerment Schemes","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Swawlamban as the scheme.\nIt was aimed at providing women with training and skills in traditional and non-traditional occupations.\nSkill development can improve women's employment and economic independence.","revisionPoints":"Swawlamban → women-oriented skill/training scheme\nFocus → traditional and non-traditional trades.\nWomen's skill development contributes to economic empowerment.\nDistinguish welfare schemes by their target group and primary objective."},
{"no":16,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Orange Prize is awarded to a work of published fiction in English by a woman.\n2. Pulitzer Prize is awarded by the Commonwealth Foundation to a citizen of any Commonwealth country for any literary work in English.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Literature","tags":["Orange Prize","Pulitzer Prize","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies statement 1 as correct.\nThe Orange Prize for Fiction was associated with women's fiction writing in English.\nStatement 2 incorrectly describes the Pulitzer Prize and its awarding body.\nThe Pulitzer Prize is associated with the United States, not the Commonwealth Foundation.","revisionPoints":"Orange Prize → women's fiction\nPulitzer Prize → major American awards in journalism, literature and other fields.\nDo not confuse the Pulitzer Prize with Commonwealth literary awards.\nAward questions often test the award + field + eligibility + institution combination."},
{"no":17,"text":"Recently, the manuscripts of which one of the following have been included in UNESCO's Memory of the World Register?","A":"Abhidhamma Pitaka","B":"Mahabharata","C":"Ramayana","D":"Rig-Veda","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Heritage","tags":["UNESCO Memory of World Register","Rig-Veda","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies the Rig-Veda manuscripts.\nThe Memory of the World Programme seeks to preserve and provide access to documentary heritage of global significance.\nThe Rig-Veda is one of the oldest collections of sacred hymns of the Vedic tradition.","revisionPoints":"Rig-Veda → Vedic literature\nMemory of the World → documentary heritage preservation.\nVedas are important sources for understanding early Indian cultural history.\nUNESCO programmes can cover cultural, documentary and natural heritage."},
{"no":18,"text":"Selene-1, the lunar orbiter mission belongs to which one of the following?","A":"China","B":"European Union","C":"Japan","D":"USA","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Selene-1","Lunar Missions","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Selene-1 was a Japanese lunar exploration mission.\nIt was launched by JAXA, Japan's space agency.\nThe mission was also known as Kaguya.\nIt studied the Moon's origin, evolution and surface/environment.","revisionPoints":"SELENE/Kaguya → Japan\nAgency → JAXA\nMission → lunar exploration.\nCompare major Asian lunar missions by country, agency and objective."},
{"no":19,"text":"From which one of the following countries did India buy the Barak anti-missile defence systems?","A":"Israel","B":"France","C":"Russia","D":"USA","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence","tags":["Barak Anti-Missile Defence System","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Israel as the supplier.\nThe Barak system is a surface-to-air missile system used for defence against aerial threats.\nIndia and Israel have had significant defence-technology cooperation.","revisionPoints":"Barak system → Israel\nUsed for air-defence purposes.\nIndia-Israel relations include defence and technology cooperation.\nDefence questions often test system + country of origin."},
{"no":20,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Rice fields\n2. Coal mining\n3. Domestic animals\n4. Wetlands\nWhich of the above are sources of methane, a major greenhouse gas?","A":"1 and 4 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Methane","Greenhouse Gases","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Rice fields can produce methane under waterlogged conditions.\nCoal mining can release methane trapped in coal seams.\nDomestic animals, especially ruminants, produce methane through digestion.\nWetlands naturally generate methane under anaerobic conditions.\nTherefore, all four are methane sources.","revisionPoints":"Methane (CH₄) → major greenhouse gas\nRice cultivation → methane from anaerobic decomposition.\nRuminants → methane through enteric fermentation.\nWetlands → important natural methane sources.\nCoal mining can release methane into the atmosphere."},
{"no":21,"text":"What is the number of spokes in the Dharmachakra in the National Flag of India?","A":"16","B":"18","C":"22","D":"24","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"National Symbols","tags":["National Flag","Dharmachakra","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Ashoka Chakra is present at the centre of the white band of India's National Flag.\nIt has 24 spokes.\nThe Chakra is derived from the Lion Capital of Ashoka at Sarnath.","revisionPoints":"Ashoka Chakra → 24 spokes\nColour → Navy blue\nLocated in the white band of the National Flag.\nRepresents the Dharma Chakra."},
{"no":22,"text":"Which one of the following is not essentially a species of the Himalayan vegetation?","A":"Juniper","B":"Mahogany","C":"Silver fir","D":"Spruce","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Himalayan Vegetation","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Juniper, silver fir and spruce are associated with Himalayan vegetation.\nMahogany is primarily associated with tropical regions.\nTherefore, mahogany is the odd one out.","revisionPoints":"Juniper → Himalayan vegetation\nSilver fir → Himalayan conifer\nSpruce → Himalayan conifer\nMahogany → tropical vegetation."},
{"no":23,"text":"Which of the following hills are found where the Eastern Ghats and the Western Ghats meet?","A":"Anaimalai Hills","B":"Cardamom Hills","C":"Nilgiri Hills","D":"Shevoroy Hills","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Nilgiri Hills","Eastern and Western Ghats","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Nilgiri Hills are located at the meeting region of the Eastern and Western Ghats.\nThey are mainly located in Tamil Nadu, with adjoining areas of Kerala and Karnataka.\nThe Nilgiris form an important highland region of South India.","revisionPoints":"Nilgiri Hills → meeting region of Eastern & Western Ghats\nNilgiris → South India.\nImportant peak → Doddabetta.\nThe Western Ghats and Eastern Ghats have different geological and geographical characteristics."},
{"no":24,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\n1. Chambal : Narmada\n2. Sone : Yamuna\n3. Manas : Brahmaputra\nWhich of the pairs given above is/are correctly matched?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Rivers of India","Tributaries","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Manas → Brahmaputra is correctly matched.\nChambal is a tributary of the Yamuna, not Narmada.\nSone is a tributary of the Ganga, not Yamuna.\nTherefore, only pair 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Chambal → Yamuna\nSone → Ganga\nManas → Brahmaputra\nRiver tributary questions require identifying the main river and drainage basin."},
{"no":25,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Salt-water crocodile is found in the Andaman and Nicobar Islands.\n2. Shrew and tapir are found in the Western Ghats of the Malabar region.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Wildlife Distribution","Andaman and Nicobar","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies both statements as correct.\nSalt-water crocodiles occur in coastal and island habitats including the Andaman and Nicobar region.\nThe Western Ghats support a wide range of mammals, including species mentioned in the question.","revisionPoints":"Salt-water crocodile → coastal/island habitats\nAndaman & Nicobar → important biodiversity region.\nWestern Ghats → major biodiversity hotspot.\nSpecies-distribution questions often require linking animals with their specific habitats."},
{"no":26,"text":"Which one amongst the following has the largest livestock population in the world?","A":"Brazil","B":"China","C":"India","D":"USA","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Livestock Population","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies India as having the largest livestock population.\nLivestock includes animals such as cattle, buffaloes, sheep, goats and others.\nLivestock is an important component of India's rural economy.","revisionPoints":"India → largest livestock population according to the source.\nLivestock supports milk, meat, wool and agricultural activities.\nCattle and buffaloes are particularly important in India's livestock economy.\nLivestock distribution is closely related to rural livelihoods."},
{"no":27,"text":"How many High Courts in India have jurisdiction over more than one State, excluding Union Territories?","A":"2","B":"3","C":"4","D":"5","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["High Courts","Common Jurisdiction","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies four High Courts having jurisdiction over more than one State.\nA High Court may exercise jurisdiction over more than one State when Parliament provides for such an arrangement.\nThe question specifically excludes Union Territories.","revisionPoints":"High Courts are established under Article 214.\nA common High Court can serve more than one State.\nUnion Territories may also fall under a High Court's jurisdiction.\nThe number of High Courts can change over time through legislation."},
{"no":28,"text":"In which one of the following kinds of organisms is the phenomenon found wherein the female kills the male after copulation?","A":"Dragonfly","B":"Honeybee","C":"Spider","D":"Pit viper","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Animal Behaviour","Honeybee","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies honeybee as the answer.\nIn honeybees, the queen mates with drones.\nThe drone dies after mating as part of the reproductive process.","revisionPoints":"Honeybee → queen + drones\nDrone's primary biological role → reproduction.\nThe queen is the principal reproductive female in a colony.\nSocial insects show specialised reproductive roles."},
{"no":29,"text":"For which one of the following snakes is the diet mainly composed of other snakes?","A":"Krait","B":"Russell's viper","C":"Rattlesnake","D":"King cobra","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["King Cobra","Snakes","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The King cobra is primarily a snake-eating species.\nIts diet consists mainly of other snakes.\nIt is one of the largest venomous snakes in the world.","revisionPoints":"King cobra → primarily eats snakes\nScientific name → Ophiophagus hannah.\n“Ophiophagus” refers to snake-eating.\nIt is found mainly in parts of South and Southeast Asia."},
{"no":30,"text":"Which one of the following is an insectivorous plant?","A":"Passion flower plant","B":"Pitcher plant","C":"Night queen","D":"Flame of the forest","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Insectivorous Plants","Pitcher Plant","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The pitcher plant is an insectivorous plant.\nIts modified leaves form a pitcher-like structure that traps insects.\nInsects provide nutrients, particularly nitrogen, in nutrient-poor soils.","revisionPoints":"Pitcher plant → insectivorous plant\nOther examples → Nepenthes, Drosera, Venus flytrap.\nCarnivorous plants supplement nutrients by trapping organisms.\nInsectivory is an adaptation to nutrient-poor environments."},
{"no":31,"text":"Starting with the Australian Open Lawn Tennis Tournament, which one of the following is the correct chronological order of the other three major Lawn Tennis Tournaments?","A":"French Open — US Open — Wimbledon","B":"French Open — Wimbledon — US Open","C":"Wimbledon — US Open — French Open","D":"Wimbledon — French Open — US Open","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Lawn Tennis Grand Slam Tournaments","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The four Grand Slam tournaments follow this broad annual sequence:\nAustralian Open\nFrench Open\nWimbledon\nUS Open\nTherefore, after the Australian Open, the correct sequence is French Open → Wimbledon → US Open.","revisionPoints":"Australian Open → January\nFrench Open → clay\nWimbledon → grass\nUS Open → hard court\nTogether they constitute the four Grand Slam tournaments."},
{"no":32,"text":"In which one of the following is Malta located?","A":"Baltic Sea","B":"Mediterranean Sea","C":"Black Sea","D":"North Sea","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Malta","Mediterranean Sea","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Malta is an island country in the Mediterranean Sea.\nIt lies south of Italy and north of the North African coast.\nIts geographical location gives it strategic importance in the Mediterranean.","revisionPoints":"Malta → Mediterranean Sea\nCapital → Valletta\nLocated south of Italy.\nMalta is a member of the European Union."},
{"no":33,"text":"ISRO successfully conducted a rocket test using cryogenic engines in 2007. Where is the test stand used for this purpose located?","A":"Balasore","B":"Thiruvananthapuram","C":"Mahendragiri","D":"Karwar","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["ISRO","Cryogenic Engine","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Mahendragiri as the location.\nMahendragiri is in Tamil Nadu.\nIt is associated with ISRO's propulsion-testing infrastructure.","revisionPoints":"Mahendragiri → Tamil Nadu\nAssociated with ISRO propulsion/engine testing.\nCryogenic engines are important for high-performance launch vehicles.\nCryogenic propulsion uses fuels/oxidisers maintained at very low temperatures."},
{"no":34,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I — Service/Agency\nA. Foreign Intelligence ServiceB. Ministry of State SecurityC. Secret Intelligence ServiceD. The Mossad\nList-II — Country\nIsrael\nBritain\nChina\nRussia\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"A-4, B-1, C-2, D-3","B":"A-2, B-3, C-4, D-1","C":"A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1","D":"A-2, B-1, C-4, D-3","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["Intelligence Agencies of the World","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Foreign Intelligence Service → Russia\nMinistry of State Security → China\nSecret Intelligence Service → Britain\nMossad → Israel\nTherefore, option (c) provides the correct matching.","revisionPoints":"Foreign Intelligence Service → Russia\nMinistry of State Security → China\nSecret Intelligence Service (MI6) → UK\nMossad → Israel\nIntelligence-agency questions often test agency-country associations."},
{"no":35,"text":"C. Rangarajan has been actively involved in the Pan-Asian Commission addressing which one of the following issues?","A":"Promotion of tourism in the Asian region","B":"Impact of HIV/AIDS in the Asian region","C":"Promotion of free trade in the Asian region","D":"Higher technical education in universities in the Asian region","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["Pan-Asian Commission","HIV/AIDS","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source associates C. Rangarajan with the Pan-Asian Commission addressing the impact of HIV/AIDS.\nHIV/AIDS has significant public-health, social and economic consequences.\nRegional cooperation is important for addressing communicable diseases.","revisionPoints":"C. Rangarajan → Pan-Asian HIV/AIDS Commission\nHIV → Human Immunodeficiency Virus.\nAIDS → Acquired Immunodeficiency Syndrome.\nPublic-health issues can require regional and international cooperation."},
{"no":36,"text":"What is the pH level of blood of a normal person?","A":"4.5–4.6","B":"6.45–6.55","C":"7.35–7.45","D":"8.25–8.35","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Human Physiology","Blood pH","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Normal human blood is slightly alkaline.\nIts normal pH is approximately 7.35–7.45.\nMaintaining this narrow range is important for normal enzyme activity and physiological processes.","revisionPoints":"Normal blood pH → 7.35–7.45\npH below 7 → acidic.\npH above 7 → alkaline.\nThe body maintains blood pH through buffer systems, lungs and kidneys."},
{"no":37,"text":"Who among the following have held the office of Vice-President of India?\n1. Mohammad Hidayatullah\n2. Fakhruddin Ali Ahmed\n3. Neelam Sanjiva Reddy\n4. Shankar Dayal Sharma\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1, 2, 3 and 4","B":"1 and 4 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"3 and 4 only","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Union Executive","tags":["Vice Presidents of India","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Mohammad Hidayatullah served as Vice-President of India.\nShankar Dayal Sharma also served as Vice-President.\nFakhruddin Ali Ahmed served as President, not Vice-President.\nNeelam Sanjiva Reddy served as President, not Vice-President.","revisionPoints":"Mohammad Hidayatullah → Vice-President\nShankar Dayal Sharma → Vice-President\nFakhruddin Ali Ahmed → President\nNeelam Sanjiva Reddy → President\nAlways distinguish the constitutional offices of President and Vice-President."},
{"no":38,"text":"Which was the capital of Andhra State when it was made a separate State in 1953?","A":"Guntur","B":"Kurnool","C":"Nellore","D":"Warangal","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Andhra State","Linguistic Reorganization","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Andhra State was formed in 1953.\nKurnool served as its capital.\nThe creation of Andhra State followed the movement for a separate Telugu-speaking state.\nLater, Andhra Pradesh was formed in 1956.","revisionPoints":"Andhra State → 1953\nCapital → Kurnool\nFormation was closely associated with the linguistic reorganisation of states.\nAndhra Pradesh → formed in 1956 after the States Reorganisation process."},
{"no":39,"text":"Which one of the following is correct in respect of the Indian football team's performance in the Olympic Games?","A":"India has never participated in the Olympic Football Tournament","B":"India played only in the First Round","C":"India entered only up to the Quarter Finals","D":"India entered the Semi Finals","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Indian Football History","Olympics","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies semi-final participation as the correct answer.\nIndia's Olympic football history includes notable performances in the early post-independence period.\nThe Indian team achieved its best Olympic football results in the mid-20th century.","revisionPoints":"India participated in the Olympic football tournament.\nIndian football had notable international performances in the early post-independence era.\nOlympic sports questions may test historical achievements, not just recent performance.\nDistinguish Olympic football results from Asian Games results."},
{"no":40,"text":"In which one of the following States are the Namchik-Namphuk Coalfields located?","A":"Arunachal Pradesh","B":"Meghalaya","C":"Manipur","D":"Mizoram","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Coalfields of India","Arunachal Pradesh","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Namchik-Namphuk Coalfields are located in Arunachal Pradesh.\nThey are associated with the state's mineral resources.\nCoal-bearing areas are important for regional energy and industrial development.","revisionPoints":"Namchik-Namphuk → Arunachal Pradesh\nArunachal Pradesh is part of Northeast India.\nNortheast India contains several important mineral and energy resources.\nLocation-based mineral questions are important for Indian geography."},
{"no":41,"text":"Norman Ernest Borlaug, who is regarded as the father of the Green Revolution in India, is from which country?","A":"United States of America","B":"Mexico","C":"Australia","D":"New Zealand","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Norman Borlaug","Green Revolution","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Norman Ernest Borlaug was an American agricultural scientist.\nHe played a major role in developing high-yielding crop varieties and modern agricultural practices.\nHis work had a major influence on the Green Revolution.\nThe source identifies the USA as his country.","revisionPoints":"Norman Borlaug → USA\nAssociated with the Green Revolution.\nHis work focused particularly on improving wheat production.\nGreen Revolution involved HYV seeds, irrigation, fertilizers and improved agricultural technology."},
{"no":42,"text":"The Yom Kippur War was fought between which sides/countries?","A":"Turkey and Greece","B":"Serbs and Croats","C":"Israel, and Arab countries led by Egypt and Syria","D":"Iran and Iraq","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Affairs","tags":["Yom Kippur War","Middle East","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Yom Kippur War took place in 1973.\nEgypt and Syria launched a coordinated attack against Israel.\nThe conflict occurred during the broader Arab-Israeli conflict.\nIt is also known as the October War.","revisionPoints":"Yom Kippur War → 1973\nMain Arab participants → Egypt and Syria\nOpposing side → Israel\nAlso called the October War / Ramadan War."},
{"no":43,"text":"Consider the following statements about probiotic food:\n1. Probiotic food contains live bacteria which are considered beneficial to humans.\n2. Probiotic food helps in maintaining gut flora.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Probiotic Food","Gut Flora","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Probiotic foods contain live microorganisms that can provide health benefits when consumed appropriately.\nThey are associated with maintaining or supporting the balance of microorganisms in the gut.\nExamples include certain fermented foods containing beneficial cultures.","revisionPoints":"Probiotics → beneficial live microorganisms\nAssociated with gut microbiota.\nCommonly found in some fermented foods.\nDo not confuse probiotics with prebiotics, which are substances that support beneficial microorganisms."},
{"no":44,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The albedo of an object determines its visual brightness when viewed with reflected light.\n2. The albedo of Mercury is much greater than the albedo of the Earth.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Albedo","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Albedo refers to the fraction of incident radiation reflected by a surface.\nA higher albedo generally means a surface reflects more incoming light.\nMercury has a lower, not higher, albedo than Earth.\nTherefore, statement 1 is correct and statement 2 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Albedo → reflectivity of a surface\nHigh albedo → more incoming radiation reflected.\nLow albedo → more radiation absorbed.\nEarth has a relatively higher average albedo than Mercury."},
{"no":45,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I — Person\nA. Nagender SinghB. A. N. RayC. R. K. TrivediD. Ashok Desai\nList-II — Position\nChief Election Commissioner of India\nPresident, International Court of Justice\nChief Justice of India\nAttorney General of India\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"A-1 B-4 C-2 D-3","B":"A-2 B-3 C-1 D-4","C":"A-1 B-3 C-2 D-4","D":"A-2 B-4 C-1 D-3","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Chief Justice of India","Attorney General","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Nagender Singh → President, International Court of Justice\nA. N. Ray → Chief Justice of India\nR. K. Trivedi → Chief Election Commissioner\nAshok Desai → Attorney General of India\nTherefore, option (b) is the correct combination.","revisionPoints":"Nagender Singh → ICJ\nA. N. Ray → Chief Justice of India\nR. K. Trivedi → Chief Election Commissioner\nAshok Desai → Attorney General\nPersonality–office matching requires attention to the exact constitutional/institutional position."},
{"no":46,"text":"What does the Baudhayan theorem (Baudhayan Sulva Sutra) relate to?","A":"Lengths of sides of a right-angled triangle","B":"Calculation of the value of pi","C":"Logarithmic calculations","D":"Normal distribution curve","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["Baudhayan Theorem","Ancient Indian Mathematics","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Baudhayan Sulva Sutra contains a geometric rule corresponding to the Pythagorean theorem.\nIt relates the sides of a right-angled triangle.\nThe Sulva Sutras are ancient Indian texts dealing with geometric constructions.","revisionPoints":"Baudhayan Sulva Sutra → geometry\nRelated to the right-angled triangle relationship.\nSulva Sutras are associated with construction of ritual altars.\nAncient Indian mathematics included significant geometric knowledge."},
{"no":47,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. Continental Drift : Edwin Hubble\n2. Expansion of Universe : Alfred Wegener\n3. Photoelectric Effect : Albert Einstein\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"2 and 3 only","B":"3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1 only","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Continental Drift","Photoelectric Effect","Scientists and Theories","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Albert Einstein → Photoelectric Effect is correctly matched.\nContinental Drift → Alfred Wegener, not Edwin Hubble.\nExpansion of the Universe → associated with Edwin Hubble, not Alfred Wegener.\nTherefore, only statement 3 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Continental Drift → Alfred Wegener\nExpansion of Universe → Edwin Hubble\nPhotoelectric Effect → Albert Einstein\nMatching scientist with the correct theory/discovery is a frequent Prelims theme."},
{"no":48,"text":"Which of the following minerals are found naturally in the State of Chhattisgarh?\n1. Bauxite\n2. Dolomite\n3. Iron ore\n4. Tin\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Mineral Resources","Chhattisgarh","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies all four minerals as occurring naturally in Chhattisgarh.\nChhattisgarh is an important mineral-producing State.\nIts mineral resources support mining and associated industries.","revisionPoints":"Chhattisgarh → mineral-rich State\nImportant minerals include:\nBauxite\nDolomite\nIron ore\nTin\nMineral-resource questions should be studied through State–mineral mapping."},
{"no":49,"text":"On which one of the following rivers is the Tehri Hydropower Complex located?","A":"Alaknanda","B":"Bhagirathi","C":"Dhauliganga","D":"Mandakini","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Tehri Hydropower Complex","Bhagirathi","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Tehri Hydropower Complex is located on the Bhagirathi River.\nIt is situated in Uttarakhand.\nThe project is an important component of India's hydropower infrastructure.","revisionPoints":"Tehri Project → Bhagirathi River\nLocation → Uttarakhand\nBhagirathi is one of the principal headstreams of the Ganga.\nRiver-valley projects are important for both power generation and water management."},
{"no":50,"text":"Which one of the following cities is nearest to the Equator?","A":"Colombo","B":"Jakarta","C":"Manila","D":"Singapore","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Cities Nearest Equator","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Equator passes very close to Singapore.\nSingapore is located just north of the Equator.\nJakarta is also near the Equator but is farther away than Singapore under the source's comparison.","revisionPoints":"Singapore → very close to Equator\nEquator → 0° latitude.\nEquatorial regions generally experience high temperatures throughout the year.\nLatitude is measured north or south of the Equator."},
{"no":51,"text":"Which of the following pairs are correctly matched?\n1. Kapildhara Falls : Godavari\n2. Jog Falls : Sharavati\n3. Sivasamudram Falls : Cauvery\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Waterfalls of India","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Jog Falls → Sharavati River is correctly matched.\nSivasamudram Falls → Cauvery River is correctly matched.\nKapildhara Falls is not associated with the Godavari in the source's matching.\nTherefore, pairs 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Jog Falls → Sharavati → Karnataka\nSivasamudram Falls → Cauvery → Karnataka\nWaterfalls are often used in UPSC questions to test river–location relationships.\nLearn major waterfalls together with their rivers and States."},
{"no":52,"text":"Which one of the following straits is nearest to the International Date Line?","A":"Malacca Strait","B":"Bering Strait","C":"Strait of Florida","D":"Strait of Gibraltar","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Bering Strait","International Date Line","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The International Date Line roughly follows the 180° meridian.\nThe Bering Strait lies close to this region.\nIt separates Russia and Alaska (USA).","revisionPoints":"Bering Strait → near International Date Line\nSeparates Russia and Alaska.\nInternational Date Line roughly follows 180° longitude, with deviations.\nDo not confuse the International Date Line with a perfectly straight 180° meridian."},
{"no":53,"text":"Which of the following pairs in respect of current power generation in India are correctly matched?\n1. Installed electricity generation capacity: 100,000 MW\n2. Electricity generation: 660 billion kWh\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Energy","tags":["Electricity Generation Capacity","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source marks neither statement as correct.\nThe figures given do not correspond to the relevant power-generation statistics for the period of the question.\nInstalled capacity and actual generation are different measures.","revisionPoints":"Installed capacity → maximum rated generating capacity\nElectricity generation → actual electricity produced\nCapacity is generally measured in MW/GW.\nElectricity generation is commonly measured in kWh/billion kWh."},
{"no":54,"text":"Which of the following pairs are correctly matched?\n1. Damanganga : Gujarat\n2. Girna : Maharashtra\n3. Pamba : Kerala\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Irrigation Projects of India","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Damanganga → Gujarat\nGirna → Maharashtra\nPamba → Kerala\nAll three pairs are correctly matched according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Damanganga → Gujarat\nGirna → Maharashtra\nPamba → Kerala\nIrrigation-project questions often require mapping project → river → State."},
{"no":55,"text":"Who among the following rejected the title of Knighthood and refused to accept a position in the Council of the Secretary of State for India?","A":"Motilal Nehru","B":"M. G. Ranade","C":"G. K. Gokhale","D":"B. G. Tilak","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["G. K. Gokhale","Knighthood","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Gopal Krishna Gokhale.\nGokhale was an important moderate nationalist and social reformer.\nHe advocated constitutional methods and gradual political reform.\nHe was associated with the Servants of India Society.","revisionPoints":"G. K. Gokhale → Moderate nationalist\nFounded the Servants of India Society in 1905.\nGandhi regarded Gokhale as an important political mentor.\nGokhale emphasised constitutional and reformist methods."},
{"no":56,"text":"During the time of which Mughal Emperor did the English East India Company establish its first factory in India?","A":"Akbar","B":"Jahangir","C":"Shahjahan","D":"Aurangzeb","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["English East India Company","Jahangir","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Jahangir.\nThe English East India Company established its early factory at Surat during Jahangir's reign.\nThis marked an important stage in the expansion of English commercial activity in India.","revisionPoints":"Jahangir → English East India Company's early factory\nImportant early centre → Surat\nEast India Company was established in 1600.\nEuropean trading companies initially came to India primarily for commercial purposes."},
{"no":57,"text":"The term “Prisoner's Dilemma” is associated with which one of the following?","A":"A technique in glass manufacture","B":"A term used in shipping industry","C":"A situation under Game Theory","D":"Name of a supercomputer","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Prisoner's Dilemma","Game Theory","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Prisoner's Dilemma is a classic problem in Game Theory.\nIt examines how individuals may make decisions when their interests are interdependent.\nIt illustrates the tension between individual rationality and collective outcomes.","revisionPoints":"Prisoner's Dilemma → Game Theory\nGame theory studies strategic decision-making.\nDecisions depend on the actions or expected actions of others.\nIt is used in economics, political science and strategic studies."},
{"no":58,"text":"During the Indian Freedom Struggle, who among the following raised an army called the “Free Indian Legion”?","A":"Lala Hardayal","B":"Rashbehari Bose","C":"Subhas Chandra Bose","D":"V. D. Savarkar","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Rashbehari Bose","Free Indian Legion","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Rashbehari Bose.\nThe Free Indian Legion was associated with efforts to organise Indian forces against British rule during World War II.\nSubhas Chandra Bose later played a major role in the Indian National Army (INA).","revisionPoints":"Rashbehari Bose → Free Indian Legion according to the source.\nSubhas Chandra Bose → Indian National Army (INA)\nRashbehari Bose played an important role in revolutionary nationalism.\nWorld War II created opportunities for anti-colonial mobilisation outside India."},
{"no":59,"text":"Who among the following suggested the reconstitution of the Viceroy's Executive Council in which all portfolios, including the War Members, were to be held by Indian leaders?","A":"Simon Commission","B":"Simla Conference","C":"Cripps Proposal","D":"Cabinet Mission","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Simla Conference","Viceroy's Executive Council","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The proposal is associated with the Simla Conference of 1945.\nIt was held under Viceroy Lord Wavell.\nThe Wavell Plan proposed reconstituting the Executive Council with Indian members.\nPolitical disagreements prevented the plan from being successfully implemented.","revisionPoints":"Simla Conference → 1945\nAssociated with Wavell Plan.\nObjective → reconstitute the Viceroy's Executive Council.\nIt preceded the Cabinet Mission of 1946."},
{"no":60,"text":"What is Agent Orange?","A":"An ace spying group trained by the Police in London","B":"A weed-killing chemical used by the US Military in the Vietnam War","C":"A technique used in advanced railway safety procedures","D":"A special chemical used in making alloy steel","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Affairs","tags":["Agent Orange","Vietnam War","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Agent Orange was a herbicidal chemical mixture used extensively during the Vietnam War.\nIt was used to destroy vegetation and expose areas where opposing forces were operating.\nIts use became associated with serious long-term environmental and health consequences.","revisionPoints":"Agent Orange → Vietnam War\nUsed as a herbicide/defoliant.\nIntended to destroy vegetation.\nIts historical use is associated with major environmental and health concerns."},
{"no":61,"text":"Which of the following countries share borders with Moldova?\n1. Ukraine\n2. Romania\n3. Belarus\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Moldova","Eastern Europe","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Moldova shares its borders with Ukraine and Romania.\nIt does not share a border with Belarus.\nMoldova is a landlocked country in Eastern Europe.","revisionPoints":"Moldova → Eastern Europe\nNeighbours → Romania and Ukraine\nCapital → Chișinău\nMoldova is landlocked."},
{"no":62,"text":"India is a member of which of the following?\n1. Asian Development Bank\n2. Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation\n3. Colombo Plan\n4. Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development (OECD)\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below:","A":"1 and 3 only","B":"2 and 4 only","C":"1, 2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["ADB","APEC","Colombo Plan","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"India is a member of the Asian Development Bank (ADB).\nIndia is also associated with the Colombo Plan.\nIndia is not a member of APEC.\nIndia is not a member of the OECD.","revisionPoints":"India → ADB member\nIndia → Colombo Plan member\nIndia → not an APEC member according to the source.\nIndia → not an OECD member according to the source."},
{"no":63,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Ajman is one of the seven Emirates of the UAE.\n2. Ras al-Khaimah was the last Sheikhdom to join the UAE.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UAE Emirates","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Ajman is one of the seven emirates forming the United Arab Emirates.\nRas al-Khaimah was the last of the seven emirates to join the federation.\nThe UAE was established in 1971.","revisionPoints":"UAE → 7 emirates\nAjman → one of the seven.\nRas al-Khaimah → joined the federation last.\nOther emirates include Abu Dhabi, Dubai, Sharjah, Fujairah and Umm Al Quwain."},
{"no":64,"text":"Which one of the following is the largest Lok Sabha constituency area-wise?","A":"Kangra","B":"Ladakh","C":"Kachchh","D":"Bhilwara","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Union Legislature","tags":["Lok Sabha Constituencies","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Ladakh as the largest Lok Sabha constituency by geographical area.\nLadakh is a sparsely populated, high-altitude region.\nLarge geographical area does not necessarily mean large population.","revisionPoints":"Ladakh → largest Lok Sabha constituency area-wise in the source's context.\nElectoral constituencies can differ greatly in geographical size.\nPopulation distribution and geographical area are separate concepts.\nLadakh is characterised by high altitude and arid/cold desert conditions."},
{"no":65,"text":"For India, China, the UK and the USA, which one of the following is the correct sequence of the median age of their populations?","A":"China < India < UK < USA","B":"India < China < USA < UK","C":"China < India < USA < UK","D":"India < China < UK < USA","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Median Age","Demographics","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies option (b).\nMedian age divides a population into two equal halves by age.\nA lower median age generally indicates a relatively younger population.\nIn the source's data, India has the lowest median age among the four.","revisionPoints":"Median age → age dividing population into two equal groups\nLower median age → relatively younger population.\nHigher median age → relatively older population.\nDemographic structure affects labour supply, dependency and social policy."},
{"no":66,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I — Person\nA. Sabyasachi MukherjeeB. Aniruddha BahalC. Vikram AkulaD. Yusuf Hamied\nList-II — Area\nMicrofinance loans\nInvestigative journalism\nFashion designing\nPharmaceuticals\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"A-1 B-2 C-3 D-4","B":"A-3 B-4 C-1 D-2","C":"A-1 B-4 C-3 D-2","D":"A-3 B-2 C-1 D-4","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Regulation","tags":["Microfinance","Business Personalities","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Sabyasachi Mukherjee → Fashion designing\nAniruddha Bahal → Investigative journalism\nVikram Akula → Microfinance\nYusuf Hamied → Pharmaceuticals\nTherefore, option (b) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Sabyasachi Mukherjee → Fashion\nAniruddha Bahal → Investigative journalism\nVikram Akula → Microfinance\nYusuf Hamied → Pharmaceuticals"},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Justice V. R. Krishna Iyer was the Chief Justice of India.\n2. Justice V. R. Krishna Iyer is considered one of the progenitors of Public Interest Litigation (PIL) in the Indian judicial system.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["Justice V. R. Krishna Iyer","Public Interest Litigation","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source marks statement 2 as correct.\nJustice V. R. Krishna Iyer was an influential judge associated with judicial activism and access to justice.\nHe was not Chief Justice of India.\nHis judicial philosophy contributed to the development of PIL in India.","revisionPoints":"V. R. Krishna Iyer → judicial reform and PIL\nAssociated with expansion of access to justice.\nPIL allows issues affecting broader sections of society to reach constitutional courts.\nHe served as a Supreme Court judge, not Chief Justice of India."},
{"no":68,"text":"Ebrahim Alkazi is an eminent personality in which one of the following areas?","A":"Heart surgery","B":"Civil aviation","C":"Theatre training","D":"Transport engineering","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Performing Arts","tags":["Ebraham Alkazi","Theatre","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Ebrahim Alkazi was a major figure in Indian theatre.\nHe was known particularly for his contribution to theatre direction and training.\nHe played an important role in modern Indian theatre education.","revisionPoints":"Ebrahim Alkazi → Theatre\nAssociated with theatre training and direction.\nImportant figure in the development of modern Indian theatre.\nPersonality–field matching is a recurring UPSC theme."},
{"no":69,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Chikmagalur is well-known for sugar production.\n2. Mandya is well-known as a coffee-producing region.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Coffee","Sugar Producing Regions","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source marks both statements as incorrect.\nChikmagalur is particularly associated with coffee production, not sugar.\nMandya is well known for sugarcane cultivation and sugar production, not primarily coffee.","revisionPoints":"Chikmagalur → coffee\nMandya → sugarcane/sugar\nKarnataka is a major coffee-producing State.\nAgricultural regions can be identified through their major commercial crops."},
{"no":70,"text":"Bimbavati Devi is a well-known dancer of which type of dance?","A":"Manipuri","B":"Bharat Natyam","C":"Kuchipudi","D":"Odissi","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Dance","tags":["Manipuri Dance","Bimbavati Devi","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Bimbavati Devi is associated with Manipuri dance.\nManipuri is one of India's classical dance traditions.\nIt originated in Manipur in northeastern India.","revisionPoints":"Bimbavati Devi → Manipuri\nManipuri → Manipur\nIndian classical dances have distinct regional traditions.\nImportant classical dances include Bharatanatyam, Kathak, Kathakali, Kuchipudi, Manipuri, Odissi and Mohiniyattam."},
{"no":71,"text":"How is Sarosh Zaiwalla well-known?","A":"A leading theatre personality","B":"A leading wildlife photographer","C":"A leading international legal expert","D":"A leading orthopaedic surgeon","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Personalities","tags":["Sarosh Zaiwalla","International Legal Expert","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Sarosh Zaiwalla is associated with international legal practice.\nHe became known for his work in international law and arbitration.\nThe source identifies him as an international legal expert.","revisionPoints":"Sarosh Zaiwalla → International law\nAssociated with international legal practice.\nSuch questions test knowledge of contemporary personalities and their professional fields.\nDistinguish legal professionals from personalities in other public fields."},
{"no":72,"text":"Where are Tapovan and Vishnugarh Hydroelectric Projects located?","A":"Madhya Pradesh","B":"Uttar Pradesh","C":"Uttarakhand","D":"Rajasthan","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Tapovan Vishnugarh","Uttarakhand Hydro Projects","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Uttarakhand as the location.\nThese are hydroelectric projects associated with the Himalayan river system.\nUttarakhand has substantial hydropower potential because of its mountainous terrain and rivers.","revisionPoints":"Tapovan–Vishnugarh → Uttarakhand\nHimalayan rivers provide significant hydropower potential.\nHydropower depends on water availability and elevation difference.\nUttarakhand has numerous hydroelectric projects."},
{"no":73,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I — Author\nA. BankimchandraB. Dinabandhu MitraC. Premchand\nList-II — Work\nShatranj Ke Khilari\nDebi Chaudhurani\nNil-Darpan\nChandrakanta\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"A-2 B-4 C-1","B":"A-3 B-4 C-2","C":"A-2 B-3 C-1","D":"A-3 B-1 C-4","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Literature","tags":["Bengali Literature","Authors and Works","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Bankimchandra Chattopadhyay → Debi Chaudhurani\nDinabandhu Mitra → Nil-Darpan\nPremchand → Shatranj Ke Khilari\nTherefore, option (c) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Bankimchandra → Debi Chaudhurani\nDinabandhu Mitra → Nil-Darpan\nPremchand → Shatranj Ke Khilari\nNil-Darpan is associated with the indigo cultivation controversy."},
{"no":74,"text":"Who among the following gave a systematic critique of the moderate politics of the Indian National Congress in a series of articles entitled New Lamps for Old?","A":"Aurobindo Ghosh","B":"R. C. Dutt","C":"Syed Ahmad Khan","D":"Viraraghavachari","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Aurobindo Ghosh","New Lamps for Old","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Aurobindo Ghosh wrote the series New Lamps for Old.\nThe articles criticised the methods and political approach of the early Congress moderates.\nHe later became an important figure associated with assertive nationalism.","revisionPoints":"New Lamps for Old → Aurobindo Ghosh\nCriticism directed at moderate Congress politics.\nAurobindo later became associated with revolutionary nationalism.\nEarly Congress debate involved differences between moderates and assertive nationalists."},
{"no":75,"text":"Who among the following used the phrase “Un-British” to criticize English colonial control of India?","A":"Anandmohan Bose","B":"Badruddin Tyabji","C":"Dadabhai Naoroji","D":"Pherozeshah Mehta","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Dadabhai Naoroji","Un-British Rule","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Dadabhai Naoroji used the term “Un-British” in his critique of British rule.\nHe argued that British economic policies resulted in a drain of wealth from India.\nHis ideas formed an important part of early nationalist economic criticism.","revisionPoints":"Dadabhai Naoroji → Drain Theory\nAssociated phrase → “Un-British Rule”\nHe analysed the economic impact of colonialism.\nHis work contributed to the development of economic nationalism in India."},
{"no":76,"text":"Among the Indian languages, which one is spoken by the maximum number of people in the world after Hindi?","A":"Telugu","B":"Tamil","C":"Bengali","D":"Malayalam","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Languages","tags":["Indian Languages","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Bengali as the answer.\nBengali is widely spoken in India and Bangladesh.\nIt is one of the major languages of the Indian subcontinent.","revisionPoints":"Bengali → major language of India and Bangladesh\nIt is an Indo-Aryan language.\nBengali has a large number of native speakers.\nLanguage questions may involve language family, geographical distribution and speakers."},
{"no":77,"text":"Who among the following discovered heavy water?","A":"Heinrich Hertz","B":"H. C. Urey","C":"G. Mendel","D":"Joseph Priestley","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Heavy Water","H. C. Urey","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Harold C. Urey discovered deuterium, the isotope of hydrogen associated with heavy water.\nHeavy water is D₂O, where deuterium replaces ordinary hydrogen.\nUrey received the Nobel Prize in Chemistry in 1934.","revisionPoints":"H. C. Urey → deuterium\nHeavy water → D₂O\nDeuterium → heavy isotope of hydrogen.\nHeavy water has applications in nuclear reactors."},
{"no":78,"text":"Kim Dae-jung won the Nobel Prize for Peace. He is from which country?","A":"Vietnam","B":"Cambodia","C":"South Korea","D":"Japan","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Affairs","tags":["Kim Dae-jung","South Korea","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Kim Dae-jung was a South Korean political leader.\nHe received the Nobel Peace Prize in 2000.\nThe award recognised his efforts concerning democracy and reconciliation, particularly in relation to the Korean Peninsula.","revisionPoints":"Kim Dae-jung → South Korea\nNobel Peace Prize → 2000\nAssociated with efforts toward inter-Korean reconciliation.\nNobel questions often test the person–country–year–field combination."},
{"no":79,"text":"The Ogaden region has been a source of conflict between which countries?","A":"Morocco and Algeria","B":"Nigeria and Cameroon","C":"Angola and Zambia","D":"Ethiopia and Somalia","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Affairs","tags":["Ogaden","Ethiopia","Somalia","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Ogaden region lies in eastern Ethiopia.\nIt has a predominantly Somali-speaking population.\nTerritorial and ethnic issues have contributed to conflict between Ethiopia and Somalia.","revisionPoints":"Ogaden → Ethiopia\nConflict → Ethiopia–Somalia\nLocated in the Horn of Africa.\nThe region is also known as the Somali Region of Ethiopia."},
{"no":80,"text":"Near which one of the following cities are Palitana Temples located?","A":"Bhavnagar","B":"Mount Abu","C":"Nasik","D":"Ujjain","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Architecture","tags":["Palitana Temples","Jain Temples","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Palitana Temples are located near Bhavnagar, Gujarat.\nThey are situated on Shatrunjaya Hill.\nPalitana is one of the most important pilgrimage sites of Jainism.","revisionPoints":"Palitana Temples → Gujarat\nNearby city → Bhavnagar\nLocation → Shatrunjaya Hill\nMajor significance → Jain pilgrimage centre."},
{"no":81,"text":"Which country won the Euro Football Tournament in 2004?","A":"Italy","B":"France","C":"Greece","D":"Portugal","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Euro Football Championship 2004","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Greece won UEFA Euro 2004.\nGreece defeated Portugal 1–0 in the final.\nThe tournament was hosted by Portugal.","revisionPoints":"Euro 2004 → Greece\nFinal → Greece vs Portugal\nHost country → Portugal\nGreece's victory was its first European Championship title."},
{"no":82,"text":"Which of the following planets lie between Mars and Uranus?","A":"Earth and Jupiter","B":"Jupiter and Saturn","C":"Saturn and Neptune","D":"Venus and Earth","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Solar System","Planetary Order","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The order of planets from the Sun is:\nMercury\nVenus\nEarth\nMars\nJupiter\nSaturn\nUranus\nNeptune\nTherefore, Jupiter and Saturn lie between Mars and Uranus.","revisionPoints":"Mars → 4th planet\nJupiter → 5th\nSaturn → 6th\nUranus → 7th\nNeptune → 8th\nJupiter is the largest planet in the Solar System."},
{"no":83,"text":"Who succeeded her husband as President of Argentina?","A":"Michelle Bachelet","B":"Cristina Kirchner","C":"Dilma Rousseff","D":"Laura Chinchilla","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Affairs","tags":["Cristina Kirchner","Argentina","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Cristina Fernández de Kirchner succeeded her husband, Néstor Kirchner, as President of Argentina.\nShe became President in 2007.\nShe served two presidential terms.","revisionPoints":"Cristina Kirchner → Argentina\nPredecessor → Néstor Kirchner\nBecame President → 2007\nArgentina → South America."},
{"no":84,"text":"Who was the Viceroy of India when the Rowlatt Act was passed?","A":"Lord Irwin","B":"Lord Reading","C":"Lord Chelmsford","D":"Lord Wavell","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Rowlatt Act","Lord Chelmsford","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Rowlatt Act was passed in 1919.\nThe Viceroy of India at that time was Lord Chelmsford.\nThe Act extended wartime emergency measures and allowed detention without ordinary judicial safeguards.\nGandhi launched a nationwide agitation against the Act.","revisionPoints":"Rowlatt Act → 1919\nViceroy → Lord Chelmsford\nAlso known as the Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act.\nRowlatt Satyagraha → important phase of Gandhi's mass politics."},
{"no":85,"text":"In 2007, an earthquake caused radioactive water leakage from the world's largest nuclear power plant. In which country did this occur?","A":"Russia","B":"France","C":"Japan","D":"USA","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Energy","tags":["Nuclear Plant Accident","Japan","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The event occurred in Japan in 2007.\nThe earthquake affected the Kashiwazaki-Kariwa Nuclear Power Plant.\nRadioactive material leakage was reported following the earthquake.","revisionPoints":"Country → Japan\nNuclear plant → Kashiwazaki-Kariwa\n2007 earthquake → caused disruption and radioactive-water leakage.\nJapan is highly exposed to earthquakes and associated hazards."},
{"no":86,"text":"What approximately is the percentage of India's population above 65 years of age?","A":"5–6%","B":"10–12%","C":"15–16%","D":"20–22%","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Population Demographics","Elderly Population","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source gives 5–6% as the approximate proportion.\nThis represents the elderly population in the demographic structure considered by the question.\nThe proportion of elderly people is important for analysing dependency and social security requirements.","revisionPoints":"Population above 65 → approximately 5–6% in the source.\nAge structure helps assess dependency ratio.\nAgeing populations increase demand for:\nHealthcare\nSocial security\nElder-care services\nDemographic percentages vary with the census/year considered."},
{"no":87,"text":"Which spacecraft was designed to study Saturn and its moons?","A":"Voyager","B":"Cassini","C":"Galileo","D":"Magellan","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["Cassini Spacecraft","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Cassini was a spacecraft mission dedicated to studying Saturn, its rings and moons.\nIt was a joint mission involving NASA, ESA and the Italian Space Agency.\nCassini entered orbit around Saturn in 2004.","revisionPoints":"Cassini → Saturn\nStudied:\nSaturn\nRings\nMoons\nMajor discovery → evidence of a subsurface ocean on Enceladus.\nCassini mission ended in 2017."},
{"no":88,"text":"Where is the ITER project located?","A":"Northern Spain","B":"Southern France","C":"Eastern Germany","D":"Western Italy","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["ITER Project","Nuclear Fusion","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"ITER is located at Saint-Paul-lès-Durance in southern France.\nITER is an international project focused on demonstrating the feasibility of fusion energy.\nIt involves multiple international partners.","revisionPoints":"ITER → Southern France\nField → Nuclear fusion\nObjective → demonstrate large-scale controlled fusion.\nFusion uses the energy released when light atomic nuclei combine."},
{"no":89,"text":"What is the primary objective of NASA's THEMIS mission?","A":"Study the Earth's magnetic field at the equator","B":"Study solar flares","C":"Study the colourful display of high-latitude skies","D":"Study the surface of Mars","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["THEMIS Mission","NASA","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies the colourful high-latitude sky phenomenon as the objective.\nThis refers particularly to auroras.\nAuroras are associated with interactions between charged particles from the Sun and Earth's magnetosphere.","revisionPoints":"THEMIS → NASA mission\nFocus → Earth's magnetosphere\nHigh-latitude sky displays → auroras\nAuroras are associated with charged particles interacting with the upper atmosphere."},
{"no":90,"text":"Which one of the following is NOT included in the UNESCO World Network of Biosphere Reserves?","A":"Nilgiri","B":"Kanchenjunga","C":"Gulf of Mannar","D":"Nanda Devi","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Biosphere Reserves","UNESCO World Network","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"According to the source, Kanchenjunga was not included in the UNESCO World Network of Biosphere Reserves at the time of the question.\nNilgiri, Gulf of Mannar and Nanda Devi were included.\nBiosphere reserves are intended to combine conservation with sustainable use.","revisionPoints":"Nilgiri → Biosphere Reserve\nGulf of Mannar → Biosphere Reserve\nNanda Devi → Biosphere Reserve\nBiosphere reserves involve:\nConservation\nSustainable development\nResearch/monitoring"},
{"no":91,"text":"Which two countries have the largest population after China and India?","A":"USA and Indonesia","B":"USA and Brazil","C":"Indonesia and Brazil","D":"Russia and Pakistan","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["World Population Ranking","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"In the population ranking considered by the source:\nChina → first\nIndia → second\nUSA → third\nIndonesia → fourth\nTherefore, USA and Indonesia follow China and India.","revisionPoints":"Population rankings depend on the year/data source.\nSource sequence:\nChina\nIndia\nUSA\nIndonesia\nPopulation size influences:\nLabour force\nResource demand\nMarkets\nPublic policy"},
{"no":92,"text":"Which State had the highest percentage of rural population according to Census 2001?","A":"Himachal Pradesh","B":"Bihar","C":"Uttar Pradesh","D":"Madhya Pradesh","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Rural Population","Census 2001","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Himachal Pradesh.\nRural population percentage measures the proportion of people living in rural areas relative to the total population.\nA high rural share does not necessarily mean the State has the largest rural population in absolute numbers.","revisionPoints":"Census reference → 2001\nHighest rural population percentage among the given options → Himachal Pradesh\nPercentage and absolute number are different demographic measures.\nRural population distribution is influenced by settlement patterns and urbanisation."},
{"no":93,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I — Organisation/Institution\nA. Amnesty InternationalB. Commonwealth of Independent StatesC. Danube CommissionD. European Space Agency\nList-II\nViskuli\nPeter Benenson\nParis\nBelgrade Convention\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"A-2 B-1 C-4 D-3","B":"A-1 B-2 C-3 D-4","C":"A-2 B-4 C-1 D-3","D":"A-3 B-1 C-4 D-2","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["Amnesty International","CIS","Danube Commission","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Amnesty International → Peter Benenson\nCIS → Viskuli\nDanube Commission → Belgrade Convention\nEuropean Space Agency → Paris","revisionPoints":"Amnesty International → founded by Peter Benenson\nCIS → associated with the Viskuli agreement\nDanube Commission → Belgrade Convention\nEuropean Space Agency → headquarters in Paris."},
{"no":94,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I — Person\nA. K. P. SinghB. Vikram PanditC. Roopa FarookiD. Kishore Biyani\nList-II — Area\nBanking\nFiction writing\nReal estate\nRetail business\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"A-1 B-2 C-3 D-4","B":"A-3 B-1 C-2 D-4","C":"A-2 B-3 C-4 D-1","D":"A-4 B-3 C-1 D-2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Regulation","tags":["Business Personalities","Banking Retail","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"K. P. Singh → Real estate\nVikram Pandit → Banking\nRoopa Farooki → Fiction writing\nKishore Biyani → Retail business","revisionPoints":"K. P. Singh → Real estate\nVikram Pandit → Banking\nRoopa Farooki → Fiction\nKishore Biyani → Retail business"},
{"no":95,"text":"The Rio+10 World Summit on Sustainable Development was held in:","A":"Brazil","B":"Germany","C":"South Africa","D":"Australia","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["World Summit on Sustainable Development","Rio+10","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The World Summit on Sustainable Development was held in Johannesburg, South Africa, in 2002.\nIt was commonly called Rio+10 because it occurred ten years after the 1992 Rio Earth Summit.\nSustainable development was the central theme.","revisionPoints":"Rio Earth Summit → 1992\nRio+10 → 2002\nLocation → Johannesburg, South Africa\nMajor focus → sustainable development\nIt followed the 1992 UN Conference on Environment and Development."},
{"no":96,"text":"Consider the following statements about the Clean Development Mechanism (CDM):\n1. It is one of the mechanisms under the Kyoto Protocol.\n2. Projects under CDM pertain only to Annex-I countries.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["Clean Development Mechanism","Kyoto Protocol","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"According to the source's answer key, both statements are treated as correct.\nCDM is one of the flexible mechanisms established under the Kyoto Protocol.\nIt relates to emissions-reduction projects and carbon credits.","revisionPoints":"CDM → Kyoto Protocol\nKyoto Protocol deals with greenhouse-gas emissions.\nCDM was designed to facilitate emission-reduction projects.\nImportant climate mechanisms also include Joint Implementation and International Emissions Trading."},
{"no":97,"text":"Which of the following cities had a population of more than one million according to Census 2001?\n1. Ludhiana\n2. Kochi\n3. Surat\n4. Nagpur\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 3 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Million-Plus Cities","Census 2001","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"According to the source, all four cities had populations exceeding one million in Census 2001.\nSuch cities are classified as million-plus urban agglomerations/cities in the relevant demographic context.","revisionPoints":"Ludhiana → million-plus\nKochi → million-plus\nSurat → million-plus\nNagpur → million-plus\nCensus-based questions require attention to the specific census year."},
{"no":98,"text":"Which of the following is/are Public Sector Undertakings of the Government of India?\n1. Balmer Lawrie\n2. Dredging Corporation of India\n3. Educational Consultants of India\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Public Sector Undertakings","tags":["Central Public Sector Enterprises","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies all three as Public Sector Undertakings of the Government of India.\nThey operate in different sectors:\nBalmer Lawrie → diversified industrial/service activities\nDredging Corporation of India → dredging services\nEducational Consultants India → educational consultancy","revisionPoints":"Balmer Lawrie → PSU\nDredging Corporation of India → PSU\nEducational Consultants India → PSU\nPublic-sector enterprises operate under government ownership/control in different forms."},
{"no":99,"text":"Which Constitutional Amendment added four languages to the Eighth Schedule, taking the total number of languages to 22?","A":"71st Amendment","B":"73rd Amendment","C":"92nd Amendment","D":"86th Amendment","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["92nd Constitutional Amendment","Eighth Schedule","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The 92nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 2003 added four languages to the Eighth Schedule.\nThe four languages were:\nBodo\nDogri\nMaithili\nSantali\nThis increased the number of scheduled languages from 18 to 22.","revisionPoints":"92nd Amendment → 2003\nAdded:\nBodo\nDogri\nMaithili\nSantali\nEighth Schedule → 22 languages after the amendment.\nEighth Schedule concerns languages recognised for constitutional purposes."},
{"no":100,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding the Constituent Assembly of India:\n1. The Constituent Assembly was elected on the basis of universal adult franchise.\n2. The minimum age for becoming a member of the Constituent Assembly was 25 years.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["State Legislative Assembly","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect because the Constituent Assembly was not elected through universal adult franchise.\nIts members were largely elected indirectly by the provincial legislative assemblies.\nStatement 2 is correct according to the source.\nThe Assembly began its work in December 1946.","revisionPoints":"Constituent Assembly → formed under the Cabinet Mission Plan, 1946.\nMembers were indirectly elected by provincial legislatures.\nPrincely States had nominated representatives.\nFirst meeting → 9 December 1946\nPresident of Constituent Assembly → Dr. Rajendra Prasad\nConstitutional Adviser → B. N. Rau"},
{"no":101,"text":"Which of the following is/are included in the Directive Principles of State Policy?\n1. Prohibition of traffic in human beings and forced labour\n2. Prohibition of consumption except for medicinal purposes of intoxicating drinks and other drugs injurious to health\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Directive Principles of State Policy","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Statement 1 is not a Directive Principle; prohibition of trafficking and forced labour is a Fundamental Right under Article 23.\nStatement 2 is a Directive Principle under Article 47.\nArticle 47 directs the State to work towards prohibition of intoxicating drinks and harmful drugs, except for medicinal purposes.","revisionPoints":"Article 23 → Fundamental Right\nArticle 23 → trafficking and forced labour prohibited.\nArticle 47 → Directive Principle\nArticle 47 → prohibition of intoxicating drinks/drugs injurious to health.\nDPSPs are contained in Part IV of the Constitution."},
{"no":102,"text":"How is the United Nations Monetary and Financial Conference, wherein agreements were signed to set up IBRD, GATT and IMF, commonly known?","A":"Bandung Conference","B":"Bretton Woods Conference","C":"Versailles Conference","D":"Yalta Conference","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["Bretton Woods Conference","IBRD","IMF","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The conference was held at Bretton Woods, New Hampshire, USA, in 1944.\nIt laid the foundation for the post-World War II international financial system.\nThe IMF and IBRD emerged from the Bretton Woods framework.","revisionPoints":"Bretton Woods Conference → 1944\nLocation → USA\nIMF → International Monetary Fund\nIBRD → International Bank for Reconstruction and Development\nGATT → General Agreement on Tariffs and Trade."},
{"no":103,"text":"Where was the first desalination plant in India to produce one lakh litres of freshwater per day based on the low-temperature thermal desalination principle commissioned?","A":"Kavaratti","B":"Port Blair","C":"Mangalore","D":"Valsad","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Desalination","Low Temperature Thermal Desalination","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Kavaratti as the location.\nKavaratti is part of Lakshadweep.\nDesalination converts saline seawater into freshwater.","revisionPoints":"Kavaratti → Lakshadweep\nDesalination → removal of salts from seawater/brackish water.\nLow-temperature thermal desalination uses differences in temperature and vapour pressure.\nIsland territories can particularly benefit from desalination because of limited freshwater resources."},
{"no":104,"text":"Which of the following pairs are correctly matched?\n1. National Centre for Cell Science : Mysore\n2. Centre for DNA Fingerprinting and Diagnostics : Hyderabad\n3. National Brain Research Centre : Gurgaon\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Research Institutes of India","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Centre for DNA Fingerprinting and Diagnostics → Hyderabad is correctly matched.\nNational Brain Research Centre → Gurgaon is correctly matched.\nNational Centre for Cell Science is not located in Mysore.","revisionPoints":"CDFD → Hyderabad\nNBRC → Gurgaon\nInstitute-location matching is frequently tested in UPSC.\nScientific institutions may be associated with specialised areas such as genetics, genomics and neuroscience."},
{"no":105,"text":"With which one of the following is BRIT (Government of India) engaged?","A":"Railway Wagons","B":"Information Technology","C":"Isotope Technology","D":"Road Transport","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["BRIT","Isotope Technology","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"BRIT stands for Board of Radiation & Isotope Technology.\nIt is associated with the use of radioisotopes and radiation technology.\nIts applications include areas such as medicine, industry and agriculture.","revisionPoints":"BRIT → Isotope Technology\nRadioisotopes have applications in:\nMedicine\nIndustry\nAgriculture\nResearch\nRadiation technology is an important application of nuclear science."},
{"no":106,"text":"Which one of the following is the correct sequence in respect of the Roman numerals C, D, L and M?","A":"C > D > L > M","B":"M > L > D > C","C":"M > D > C > L","D":"L > C > D > M","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["Roman Numerals","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Roman numeral values are:\nM = 1000\nD = 500\nC = 100\nL = 50\nTherefore: M > D > C > L.","revisionPoints":"M → 1000\nD → 500\nC → 100\nL → 50\nOther important symbols:\nX → 10\nV → 5\nI → 1"},
{"no":107,"text":"Who among the following scientists shared the Nobel Prize in Physics with his son?","A":"Max Planck","B":"Albert Einstein","C":"William Henry Bragg","D":"Enrico Fermi","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Nobel Prize in Physics","William Henry Bragg","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"William Henry Bragg and his son William Lawrence Bragg jointly received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1915.\nTheir work was related to the analysis of crystal structures using X-rays.","revisionPoints":"William Henry Bragg + William Lawrence Bragg → Nobel Physics, 1915\nField → X-ray crystallography\nThey studied the structure of crystals.\nTheir work is associated with Bragg's law."},
{"no":108,"text":"Who among the following translated the Autobiography of Madame Curie into Hindi?","A":"Atal Bihari Vajpayee","B":"Lal Bahadur Shastri","C":"Choudhary Charan Singh","D":"Govind Ballabh Pant","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Lal Bahadur Shastri","Madam Curie Autobiography","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"According to the source, Lal Bahadur Shastri translated the autobiography of Madame Curie into Hindi.\nShastri was India's second Prime Minister.\nBefore becoming Prime Minister, he was active in India's freedom movement and public life.","revisionPoints":"Lal Bahadur Shastri → second Prime Minister of India\nAssociated with the slogan “Jai Jawan, Jai Kisan.”\nMadame Curie → pioneering scientist in radioactivity.\nPersonality–work questions require careful matching."},
{"no":109,"text":"Among the following, which one has the minimum population according to Census of India, 2001?","A":"Chandigarh","B":"Mizoram","C":"Puducherry","D":"Sikkim","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["Union Territories","Census 2001","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"According to Census 2001, Sikkim had the lowest population among the given options.\nSikkim is a small Himalayan State with a relatively low population.","revisionPoints":"Census reference → 2001\nSikkim → minimum population among the given options.\nSikkim → Himalayan State.\nPopulation size should be distinguished from population density."},
{"no":110,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding a jet engine and a rocket:\n1. A jet engine uses the surrounding air for its oxygen supply and so is unsuitable for motion in space.\n2. A rocket carries its own supply of oxygen in the gas form, and fuel.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Jet Engine","Rocket Propulsion","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"A jet engine depends on atmospheric oxygen for combustion.\nTherefore, ordinary air-breathing jet engines cannot operate in the vacuum of space.\nA rocket carries its oxidizer and fuel, allowing it to operate without atmospheric oxygen.","revisionPoints":"Jet engine → depends on atmospheric oxygen.\nRocket → carries fuel + oxidizer.\nRockets can operate in space/vacuum.\nRocket propulsion follows the principle of conservation of momentum."},
{"no":111,"text":"Which one of the following laser types is used in a laser printer?","A":"Dye laser","B":"Gas laser","C":"Semiconductor laser","D":"Excimer laser","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Semiconductor Laser","Laser Printer","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Laser printers commonly use a semiconductor laser to write the image pattern onto the photoreceptor drum.\nThe laser beam is controlled electronically to form the required image.","revisionPoints":"Laser printer → semiconductor laser\nLaser → Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation.\nSemiconductor lasers are compact and efficient.\nLaser technology has applications in communication, medicine, industry and printing."},
{"no":112,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. A widely used musical scale called the diatonic scale has seven frequencies.\n2. The frequency of the note Sa is 256 Hz and that of Ni is 512 Hz.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Diatonic Scale","Sound Frequency","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source considers statement 1 correct.\nA diatonic scale consists of seven notes in its basic form.\nThe specific frequencies assigned to musical notes depend on the tuning system and reference pitch, so statement 2 is not accepted in the source.","revisionPoints":"Diatonic scale → seven notes\nMusical pitch is related to frequency.\nFrequency is measured in hertz (Hz).\nDifferent tuning systems can assign different absolute frequencies to notes."},
{"no":113,"text":"What are rubies and sapphires chemically known as?","A":"Silicon dioxide","B":"Aluminium oxide","C":"Lead tetroxide","D":"Boron nitride","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Rubies and Sapphires","Aluminium Oxide","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Ruby and sapphire are varieties of corundum.\nCorundum is chemically aluminium oxide (Al₂O₃).\nTheir different colours are caused by trace impurities.","revisionPoints":"Ruby → aluminium oxide\nSapphire → aluminium oxide\nChemical formula → Al₂O₃\nRuby's red colour is mainly associated with chromium impurities.\nCorundum is a very hard mineral."},
{"no":114,"text":"Which one of the following is also called “Stranger Gas”?","A":"Argon","B":"Neon","C":"Xenon","D":"Nitrous oxide","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Xenon","Stranger Gas","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Xenon is the noble gas referred to in the source as “Stranger Gas”.\nThe name comes from the Greek word xenos, meaning stranger or foreigner.\nXenon is chemically relatively unreactive compared with many elements.","revisionPoints":"Xenon → Stranger Gas\nSymbol → Xe\nAtomic number → 54\nXenon belongs to the noble gases.\nNoble gases include helium, neon, argon, krypton, xenon and radon."},
{"no":115,"text":"A mixture of which pair of gases causes most explosions in mines?","A":"Hydrogen and oxygen","B":"Oxygen and acetylene","C":"Methane and air","D":"Carbon dioxide and methane","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Methane","Mine Explosions","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Methane can accumulate in underground coal mines.\nWhen methane mixes with air within a suitable concentration range, it becomes highly explosive.\nAn ignition source can trigger a mine explosion.","revisionPoints":"Mine explosion hazard → methane\nMethane → CH₄\nMethane is highly combustible.\nCoal mines require continuous ventilation and gas monitoring.\nMethane is also an important greenhouse gas."},
{"no":116,"text":"Which pair of metals constitutes the lightest metal and the heaviest metal, respectively?","A":"Lithium and mercury","B":"Lithium and osmium","C":"Aluminium and osmium","D":"Aluminium and mercury","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Lightest and Heaviest Metals","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Lithium is the lightest metal.\nOsmium is generally regarded as the densest/heaviest metal by density.\nThe question uses “lightest” and “heaviest” in the conventional density context.","revisionPoints":"Lithium → lightest metal\nOsmium → extremely dense/heaviest by density\nLithium symbol → Li\nOsmium symbol → Os\nDensity and atomic mass are different concepts."},
{"no":117,"text":"How much is one barrel of oil approximately equal to?","A":"131 litres","B":"159 litres","C":"257 litres","D":"321 litres","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Energy","tags":["Barrel of Oil","Unit Conversion","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"One standard petroleum barrel is approximately 159 litres.\nMore precisely, a US oil barrel is about 158.99 litres.\nIt is a standard unit used in the petroleum industry.","revisionPoints":"1 oil barrel ≈ 159 litres\nPetroleum prices are commonly quoted per barrel.\nBarrel is different from other volume units used for liquids.\nAlways distinguish an oil barrel from other historical barrel measurements."},
{"no":118,"text":"Who among the following Gandhian followers was a teacher by profession?","A":"A. N. Sinha","B":"Braj Kishore Prasad","C":"J. B. Kriplani","D":"Rajendra Prasad","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["J. B. Kripalani","Gandhian Followers","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"J. B. Kriplani was a prominent Gandhian and freedom fighter.\nHe was associated with teaching before becoming deeply involved in the national movement.\nHe later served as President of the Indian National Congress.","revisionPoints":"J. B. Kriplani → teacher + Gandhian\nFull name → Jivatram Bhagwandas Kripalani\nAssociated with the Indian freedom movement.\nServed as INC President in 1946."},
{"no":119,"text":"Which one of the following was a journal brought out by Abul Kalam Azad?","A":"Al-Hilal","B":"Comrade","C":"The Indian Sociologist","D":"Zamindar","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Al-Hilal","Abul Kalam Azad","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Maulana Abul Kalam Azad launched the journal Al-Hilal in 1912.\nIt played an important role in expressing anti-colonial nationalist ideas.\nThe British government eventually took action against the publication.","revisionPoints":"Al-Hilal → Abul Kalam Azad\nStarted → 1912\nLanguage → Urdu\nAzad → important nationalist leader and later India's first Education Minister.\nJournalism was an important tool of nationalist mobilisation."},
{"no":120,"text":"Where was the First Session of the Indian National Congress held in December 1885?","A":"Ahmadabad","B":"Bombay","C":"Calcutta","D":"Delhi","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Indian National Congress","First Session 1885","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The first session of the Indian National Congress was held in Bombay in December 1885.\nIt was presided over by Womesh Chandra Bonnerjee (W. C. Bonnerjee).\nThe Congress was founded in 1885 during the early phase of organised Indian nationalism.","revisionPoints":"First INC Session → 1885\nLocation → Bombay\nPresident → W. C. Bonnerjee\nFounder associated with organisation → A. O. Hume\nThe first session was attended by representatives from different parts of India.\nTop of Form\nBottom of Form"},
{"no":121,"text":"Which of the following pairs is/are correctly matched?\n1. Francis Collins : Mapping human genome\n2. Sergey Brin : Google Search Engine\n3. Jimmy Wales : Wikipedia\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["Human Genome","Google","Wikipedia","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Francis Collins → Human Genome Project\nSergey Brin → Google\nJimmy Wales → Wikipedia\nAll three pairs are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Francis Collins → human genome research.\nSergey Brin → co-founder of Google.\nJimmy Wales → co-founder of Wikipedia.\nHuman Genome Project → major international scientific research project."},
{"no":122,"text":"What does S&P 500 relate to?","A":"Supercomputer","B":"A new technique in e-business","C":"A new technique in bridge building","D":"An index of stocks of large companies","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["S&P 500","Stock Index","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"S&P 500 is a major stock-market index.\nIt tracks the performance of 500 large companies listed in the United States.\nIt is widely used as an indicator of the US large-cap equity market.","revisionPoints":"S&P 500 → US stock-market index\nS&P → Standard & Poor's.\nTracks large-cap companies.\nStock indices are used to measure market performance."},
{"no":123,"text":"Rajiv Gandhi National Flying Institute is being established in which State?","A":"Karnataka","B":"Maharashtra","C":"Kerala","D":"Orissa","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Infrastructure","tags":["Rajiv Gandhi National Flying Institute","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"According to the source, the Rajiv Gandhi National Flying Institute was being established in Maharashtra.\nThe institute is associated with aviation and pilot training.","revisionPoints":"Rajiv Gandhi National Flying Institute → Maharashtra\nField → aviation/flying training\nInstitute-location questions require precise State matching."},
{"no":124,"text":"Who among the following has been the Captain of the Indian team in Cricket Test Matches for the maximum number of times?","A":"Sunil Gavaskar","B":"Mansur Ali Khan Pataudi","C":"Saurav Ganguly","D":"Mohammad Azharuddin","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["Indian Cricket Captains","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source identifies Saurav Ganguly as the answer.\nHe captained India in Test cricket for a significant number of matches during his tenure.\nHe was an important figure in Indian cricket during the early 2000s.","revisionPoints":"Saurav Ganguly → Indian Test captain\nAlso known as Dada.\nHis captaincy period included several important overseas Test series.\nCricket questions should be interpreted according to the historical period of the paper."},
{"no":125,"text":"Who among the following wrote the poem Subh-e Azadi?","A":"Sahir Ludhiyanvi","B":"Faiz Ahmed Faiz","C":"Muhammad Iqbal","D":"Maulana Abul Kalam Azad","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Faiz Ahmed Faiz","Subh-e Azadi","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Subh-e Azadi was written by Faiz Ahmed Faiz.\nThe poem reflects on the experience of independence and the expectations associated with it.\nFaiz was a major Urdu poet of the 20th century.","revisionPoints":"Subh-e Azadi → Faiz Ahmed Faiz\nLanguage → Urdu\nFaiz → prominent poet and writer.\nLiterary questions can test author–work–language combinations."},
{"no":126,"text":"How can the height of a person who is six feet tall be expressed approximately in nanometres?","A":"183 × 10⁶ nanometres","B":"234 × 10⁶ nanometres","C":"183 × 10⁷ nanometres","D":"234 × 10⁷ nanometres","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Unit Conversion","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"1 foot = 0.3048 metre.\n6 feet = approximately 1.83 metres.\n1 metre = 10⁹ nanometres.\nTherefore, 1.83 m ≈ 1.83 × 10⁹ nm = 183 × 10⁷ nm.","revisionPoints":"6 feet ≈ 1.83 metres\n1 metre = 10⁹ nanometres\n1 nanometre = 10⁻⁹ metre\nCorrect conversion → 183 × 10⁷ nm."},
{"no":127,"text":"A person is sitting in a car which is at rest. The reaction from the road at each of the four wheels is R. When the car runs on a straight level road, how will the reaction at either of the front wheels vary?","A":"It will be greater than R","B":"It will be less than R","C":"It will be equal to R","D":"It shall depend on the material of the road","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Mechanics","Reaction Force","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The source gives R as the reaction at each front wheel while moving on a straight, level road.\nUnder the idealised conditions of the question, there is no additional vertical acceleration.\nTherefore, the vertical reaction remains unchanged.","revisionPoints":"Straight level motion → no vertical acceleration.\nVertical forces must remain balanced.\nRoad reaction is related to the normal force.\nThe question assumes idealised conditions."},
{"no":128,"text":"With which one of the following rivers is the Omkareshwar Project associated?","A":"Chambal","B":"Narmada","C":"Tapi","D":"Bhima","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Omkareshwar Project","Narmada","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Omkareshwar Project is associated with the Narmada River.\nIt is a major multipurpose/hydroelectric project in Madhya Pradesh.\nThe Narmada is one of India's major west-flowing rivers.","revisionPoints":"Omkareshwar Project → Narmada\nState → Madhya Pradesh\nNarmada → flows westward into the Arabian Sea.\nImportant Narmada projects include Sardar Sarovar and Omkareshwar."},
{"no":129,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The Nuclear Suppliers Group has 24 countries as its members.\n2. India is a member of the Nuclear Suppliers Group.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Organizations","tags":["Nuclear Suppliers Group","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"According to the source's 2008 context, the NSG did not have 24 members.\nIndia was not an NSG member.\nIndia has participated in discussions with the NSG but was not a member at the time of this paper.","revisionPoints":"NSG → Nuclear Suppliers Group\nPurpose → controls nuclear-related exports and prevents nuclear proliferation.\nIndia was not an NSG member in the context of this question.\nAlways consider the year of the examination for membership-based questions."},
{"no":130,"text":"In the context of Indian defence, what is 'Dhruv'?","A":"Aircraft-carrying warship","B":"Missile-carrying submarine","C":"Advanced Light Helicopter","D":"Intercontinental ballistic missile","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence","tags":["Dhruv","Advanced Light Helicopter","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Dhruv is India's Advanced Light Helicopter (ALH).\nIt was developed by Hindustan Aeronautics Limited (HAL).\nIt has both military and civilian applications.","revisionPoints":"Dhruv → Advanced Light Helicopter\nDeveloper → HAL\nUsed for:\nTransport\nReconnaissance\nSearch and rescue\nMilitary operations\nIt is a helicopter, not a missile or warship."},
{"no":131,"text":"'Hand-in-Hand 2007', a joint anti-terrorism military training, was held by officers of the Indian Army and officers of the Army of which country?","A":"China","B":"Japan","C":"Russia","D":"USA","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Bilateral Relations","tags":["Hand-in-Hand 2007","India-China Military Exercise","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Hand-in-Hand 2007 was a joint military exercise between India and China.\nIt focused on joint counter-terrorism training.\nIt was an important example of military engagement between the two countries.","revisionPoints":"Hand-in-Hand → India–China\nFirst edition → 2007\nFocus → counter-terrorism\nJoint military exercises are part of defence cooperation.\nAssertion–Reason Questions\nCode:\n(a) Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A(b) Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A(c) A is true but R is false(d) A is false but R is true\nNote: The source text does not display the highlighted answer for Questions 132–135. Therefore, the answers below are based on the standard concepts stated in the questions, rather than being presented as source-highlighted answers."},
{"no":132,"text":"Assertion (A): In the human body, the liver has an important role in fat digestion.\nReason (R): Liver produces two important fat-digesting enzymes.","A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["Liver","Fat Digestion","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The liver has an important role in fat digestion.\nHowever, the liver does not produce fat-digesting digestive enzymes as stated in the Reason.\nThe liver produces bile.\nBile salts help emulsify fats, increasing their surface area for enzyme action.\nFat-digesting enzymes such as lipase are primarily produced by the pancreas.","revisionPoints":"Liver → produces bile.\nBile → helps in emulsification of fats.\nPancreas → produces lipase.\nEmulsification is a physical process, not enzymatic digestion."},
{"no":133,"text":"Assertion (A): In the visible spectrum of light, red light is more energetic than green light.\nReason (R): The wavelength of red light is more than that of green light.","A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Light Energy","Wavelength","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Photon energy is given by E = hc/λ.\nEnergy is inversely proportional to wavelength.\nRed light has a longer wavelength than green light.\nTherefore, red light has less energy per photon than green light.","revisionPoints":"E = hc/λ\nLonger wavelength → lower photon energy.\nShorter wavelength → higher photon energy.\nVisible spectrum roughly progresses from red → violet with increasing frequency and energy."},
{"no":134,"text":"Assertion (A): Radio waves bend in a magnetic field.\nReason (R): Radio waves are electromagnetic in nature.","A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["Radio Waves","Electromagnetic Waves","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Radio waves are electromagnetic waves.\nElectromagnetic waves do not carry a net electric charge.\nTherefore, a magnetic field does not bend radio waves in the manner it bends charged particles.\nHence the Assertion is false while the Reason is true.","revisionPoints":"Radio waves → electromagnetic waves\nThey consist of oscillating electric and magnetic fields.\nElectromagnetic waves do not require a material medium.\nCharged particles, unlike electromagnetic waves, can be deflected by magnetic fields."},
{"no":135,"text":"Assertion (A): The Congress Ministries in all the provinces resigned in the year 1939.\nReason (R): The Congress did not accept the decision of the Viceroy to declare war against Germany in the context of the Second World War.","A":"Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are true but R is NOT the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["Congress Ministries Resignation 1939","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Britain declared war on Germany in 1939.\nThe Viceroy declared India to be at war without consulting Indian political leaders.\nCongress objected to this unilateral decision.\nThe Congress Ministries subsequently resigned in the provinces in 1939.","revisionPoints":"1939 → Congress Ministries resigned\nContext → beginning of Second World War\nViceroy → Lord Linlithgow\nCongress objected to India's involvement in the war without Indian consent.\nThe resignation marked an important development in the Quit India–era political background."},
{"no":136,"text":"Among the following, which one lays eggs and does not produce young ones directly?","A":"Echidna","B":"Kangaroo","C":"Porcupine","D":"Whale","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Echidna","Egg-laying Mammals","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Echidna is a monotreme.\nMonotremes are mammals that lay eggs.\nAfter hatching, the young receive milk from the mother.","revisionPoints":"Echidna → egg-laying mammal\nOther major monotreme → platypus\nMonotremes → mammals but reproduce by laying eggs.\nKangaroo and whale → give birth to live young."},
{"no":137,"text":"The release of which one of the following into ponds and wells helps in controlling mosquitoes?","A":"Crab","B":"Dogfish","C":"Gambusia fish","D":"Snail","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Gambusia Fish","Biological Mosquito Control","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Gambusia, commonly called mosquito fish, feeds on mosquito larvae.\nIntroducing it into suitable water bodies can therefore help reduce mosquito populations.\nIt is a biological method of mosquito control.","revisionPoints":"Gambusia → mosquito control\nFeeds on mosquito larvae.\nBiological control uses organisms to control pest populations.\nMosquitoes can transmit diseases such as malaria and dengue."},
{"no":138,"text":"Which Schedule of the Constitution of India contains special provisions for the administration and control of Scheduled Areas in several States?","A":"Third","B":"Fifth","C":"Seventh","D":"Ninth","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["Fifth Schedule","Scheduled Areas","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Fifth Schedule deals with the administration and control of Scheduled Areas and Scheduled Tribes in specified States.\nIt provides a special administrative framework for these areas.\nThe Governor has specific responsibilities under the Fifth Schedule.","revisionPoints":"Fifth Schedule → Scheduled Areas\nDeals with specified Scheduled Tribes and Scheduled Areas.\nSixth Schedule → tribal areas in Assam, Meghalaya, Tripura and Mizoram.\nFifth and Sixth Schedules should not be confused."},
{"no":139,"text":"Department of Border Management is a Department of which Union Ministry?","A":"Ministry of Defence","B":"Ministry of Home Affairs","C":"Ministry of Shipping, Road Transport and Highways","D":"Ministry of Environment and Forests","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Union Executive","tags":["Department of Border Management","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Department of Border Management comes under the Ministry of Home Affairs.\nIt deals with matters concerning management and development of India's international borders.\nBorder management involves infrastructure, surveillance and coordination.","revisionPoints":"Department of Border Management → Ministry of Home Affairs\nBorder management involves:\nBorder infrastructure\nSurveillance\nFencing where applicable\nCoordination with border forces\nDo not confuse border management with the broader defence responsibilities of the Ministry of Defence."},
{"no":140,"text":"For which one of the following reforms was a Commission set up under the Chairmanship of Veerappa Moily by the Government of India?","A":"Police Reforms","B":"Tax Reforms","C":"Reforms in Technical Education","D":"Administrative Reforms","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Policies","tags":["Veerappa Moily","Administrative Reforms Commission","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The Second Administrative Reforms Commission (ARC) was headed by M. Veerappa Moily.\nIt examined various aspects of India's public administration.\nIts reports covered areas such as governance, ethics, public order and administrative capacity.","revisionPoints":"Veerappa Moily → Second Administrative Reforms Commission\nARC → Administrative Reforms\nFocus → improving governance and public administration.\nImportant distinction:\nAdministrative reforms → ARC\nPolice reforms → separate reform recommendations/commissions."},
{"no":141,"text":"Elizabeth Hawley is well known for her writings on:","A":"Indian freedom movement","B":"Wildlife conservation","C":"Himalayan expeditions","D":"Indian classical music","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Personalities","tags":["Elizabeth Hawley","Himalayan Expeditions","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Elizabeth Hawley was a noted chronicler of Himalayan mountaineering.\nHer writings documented numerous mountaineering expeditions in the Himalayas.\nHer records became an important source for Himalayan climbing history.","revisionPoints":"Elizabeth Hawley → Himalayan expeditions\nAssociated particularly with mountaineering records.\nHimalayan expeditions involve major peaks such as Mount Everest.\nPersonality–field questions require precise association."},
{"no":142,"text":"How many Indian States share a coastline?","A":"7","B":"8","C":"9","D":"10","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["Coastal States of India","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"India has 9 coastal States.\nThey are located along the Arabian Sea, Bay of Bengal and Indian Ocean.\nThe question asks specifically for States, not Union Territories.","revisionPoints":"Coastal States → 9\nWestern coast → Arabian Sea.\nEastern coast → Bay of Bengal.\nCoastal Union Territories are counted separately.\nCoastal geography is important for ports, fisheries and maritime trade."},
{"no":143,"text":"James D. Watson is associated with which field?","A":"Astronomy","B":"Nuclear physics","C":"Economics","D":"Genetics","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["James D. Watson","Genetics","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"James D. Watson is associated with the discovery of the structure of DNA.\nHe and Francis Crick proposed the double-helix model of DNA.\nTheir work was based partly on experimental data from other scientists, including Rosalind Franklin and Maurice Wilkins.","revisionPoints":"James D. Watson → Genetics\nDNA → Deoxyribonucleic acid\nDNA structure → double helix\nWatson and Crick received the 1962 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine with Maurice Wilkins."},
{"no":144,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I — Board\nA. Coffee BoardB. Rubber BoardC. Tea BoardD. Tobacco Board\nList-II — Headquarters\nGuntur\nKottayam\nKolkata\nBengaluru\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"A-2 B-4 C-3 D-1","B":"A-4 B-2 C-3 D-1","C":"A-4 B-3 C-2 D-1","D":"A-3 B-2 C-4 D-1","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["Commodity Boards","Headquarters","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Coffee Board → Bengaluru\nRubber Board → Kottayam\nTea Board → Kolkata\nTobacco Board → Guntur\nTherefore, option (b) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Coffee Board → Bengaluru\nRubber Board → Kottayam\nTea Board → Kolkata\nTobacco Board → Guntur\nBoard–headquarters matching is a recurring factual area in UPSC."},
{"no":145,"text":"Which State/Union Territory has the maximum number of National Parks among the following?","A":"Andaman and Nicobar Islands","B":"Arunachal Pradesh","C":"Assam","D":"Madhya Pradesh","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["National Parks of India","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"According to the source, Andaman and Nicobar Islands has the maximum number among the given options.\nNational Parks are protected areas established for conservation of ecosystems and wildlife.","revisionPoints":"Andaman and Nicobar Islands → maximum among given options\nNational Parks → protected areas for wildlife and ecosystem conservation.\nOther important protected-area categories include:\nWildlife Sanctuaries\nBiosphere Reserves\nConservation Reserves\nProtected areas can differ in their legal provisions and management objectives."},
{"no":146,"text":"Which one of the following is NOT an ape?","A":"Gibbon","B":"Gorilla","C":"Langur","D":"Chimpanzee","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["Apes","Primates","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"Gibbons, gorillas and chimpanzees are apes.\nLangurs are monkeys.\nApes generally lack an external tail, whereas many monkeys possess tails.","revisionPoints":"Gibbon → Ape\nGorilla → Ape\nChimpanzee → Ape\nLangur → Monkey\nApes include great apes and lesser apes.\nHuman beings are also classified within the ape group."},
{"no":147,"text":"There are 9 cells arranged in a 3 × 3 square. Five identical balls are to be placed in these cells such that each row contains at least one ball. In how many ways can this be done?","A":"81","B":"90","C":"100","D":"108","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Combinatorics","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"The problem requires distributing 5 identical balls among 9 cells.\nEach of the three rows must contain at least one ball.\nApplying the appropriate distribution/counting method gives 108 arrangements according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Key conditions:\n9 cells\n5 identical balls\n3 × 3 arrangement\nEvery row → at least one ball\nThis is a combinatorics/counting problem.\n“Identical balls” means exchanging two balls does not create a new arrangement."},
{"no":148,"text":"There are 6 different letters and 6 corresponding envelopes. If the letters are placed randomly into the envelopes, what is the probability that exactly 5 letters are placed in their correct envelopes?","A":"0","B":"1/6","C":"1/3","D":"1/2","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Probability","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"If exactly 5 letters are correctly placed, the remaining sixth letter must also go into its own envelope.\nTherefore, it is impossible to have exactly 5 correct placements.\nThe possible number of correct placements can be 0, 1, 2, 3, 4 or 6, but not exactly 5.","revisionPoints":"Exactly 5 correct → impossible\nProbability → 0\nThis is based on the logic of permutations/derangements.\nIf \\(n-1\\) objects are correctly placed, the final object is automatically correctly placed as well."},
{"no":149,"text":"Two identical red balls, two identical black balls and two identical white balls are arranged in a row of four cells. No two balls of the same colour can be consecutive. In how many ways can this be done?","A":"18","B":"20","C":"24","D":"30","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Combinatorics","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"There are three colours:\n2 red\n2 black\n2 white\nFour positions are selected for the six balls according to the conditions of the question.\nThe restriction is that identical colours cannot occur consecutively.\nThe source gives 24 as the answer.","revisionPoints":"Objects of the same colour are identical.\nConsecutive identical colours are prohibited.\nCounting questions require careful attention to:\nIdentical vs different objects\nPosition restrictions\nRepetition\nAnswer according to the stated arrangement conditions → 24."},
{"no":150,"text":"How many triangles are there in the given figure?","A":"28","B":"24","C":"32","D":"36","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["Geometry","UPSC","2008"],"expl":"This is a figure-based counting problem.\nThe source gives the total number of triangles as 28.\nSuch questions require counting triangles of different sizes and orientations systematically.","revisionPoints":"Total triangles → 28\nCount systematically by:\nSmall triangles\nMedium triangles\nLarge triangles\nComposite triangles\nAvoid counting the same triangle more than once.\nFigure-based questions test visual reasoning and systematic enumeration."},
];
